  "chain": "OE not anchored [cause B] -> EDG speed switch MOV underrated for max DC voltage -> (parallel: hard ground on DCMCC2 -> oscillating ground via breaker opening) -> MOV overvoltage exposure -> speed switch internal short/overheating damage -> EDG 2 fails to start/locks out -> EDG 2 inoperable ~6 days (also -> unanalyzed condition: fire/HELB ground could affect both EDGs) [no backup asserted; redundant train 1 equipment remained Operable]",
  "golden_questions": [
    "Q1",
    "Q3",
    "Q4",
    "Q14"
  ],
  "notes": "Two-part event/reportability: initial EDG 2 failure-to-start (Sept 2020) determined retroactively (Feb 12, 2021) to have existed prior to test start, triggering 50.73(a)(2)(i)(B); a later (Oct 10, 2024) review identified an additional unanalyzed-condition failure mechanism (fire/HELB-induced ground on negative bus could affect both EDG speed switches regardless of other grounds), triggering 50.73(a)(2)(ii)(B) and this Rev 02. Event_date field carries the Feb 12, 2021 determination date per FORM-366; underlying EDG failure occurred Sept 2020. Cause code B (Design) governs despite narrative's heavy 'operating experience'/root-cause language, per rule 4. SBODG explicitly uses a separate 125VDC system and was unaffected -- not modeled as a backup since it is design-context only and not stated operable/available during this specific inoperability window for EDG 2. Breaker-to-ground-state edge modeled as LEADS_TO (functional/causal) rather than PART_OF, consistent with breaker/valve precedent in rule 7."
}